KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp17
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
unexpected expression: struct_tag
found no match for symbol 'swap'
\* identifier: std::tag-__not_<
--
Per [temp.deduct]/8 substitution failure in the return type of a
function-template candidate is silently absorbed during overload
resolution: no diagnostic should be emitted and the candidate is
simply discarded.

CBMC currently does NOT absorb these failures for libstdc++'s
`std::is_swappable<T>` (via `swap(_Tp&, _Tp&)` whose return type is
`_Require<__not_<__is_tuple_like<_Tp>>, ...>`).  The substitution
of the `__not_<__is_tuple_like<_Tp>>` template argument reaches
the type-checker with an `ID_struct_tag` expression and falls
through to the "unexpected expression" fallback in
`c_typecheck_expr.cpp:587` — leaking both the `struct_tag` dump
and a cascading "found no match for symbol 'swap'" error to
stderr.

Two observable symptoms the fix must address:
1. No diagnostic text should appear on stderr for this input.
2. The output must contain VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL (the
   `static_assert` evaluates to true).

This is the minimum-reproducer distilled from a user harness that
transitively includes <array>, which in turn instantiates
`std::array<double, N>` -> `std::__array_traits<double, N>` ->
`std::__is_swappable<double>` -> `std::__is_swappable_impl<double>`
and hits exactly this code path.
